    end
end)

-- ==================== auto farm ====================
-- A GROUND RUNNER, and that is not a stylistic choice - it is the only thing the egg
-- areas allow. Measured, in the area itself:
--
--   airborne              snapped back to the floor every ~1.5s, then KILLED at 7.3s
--   6 studs under         physics ejects you back to the surface within a second
--   25 studs under        dead in 0.1 seconds
--
-- At spawn none of that happens - 12s at 60 studs up, and 18 studs underground, both
-- fine - so the check is scoped to the egg areas. Underground travel was the plan and
-- it is simply not available: the only two states are "inside the slab", where you get
-- pushed out, and "below it", where you die instantly. There is no gap to live in.
--
-- What IS free is horizontal speed while touching the ground. So: run, do not fly, and
-- keep a small downward bias so a bump never counts as air time.
_G.FARM = _G.FARM or {}
local FM = _G.FARM
FM.On        = false
-- 300, not 220. Re-measured properly, in an egg area, along X (the axis the areas
-- actually span - the earlier test drove along Z and hit the area's boundary wall at
-- z=-295, which is why it reported a 92 studs/s "ceiling" that does not exist):
--
--   cmd 220 -> 207 studs/s actual   (94% of commanded)
--   cmd 260 -> 250                  (96%)
--   cmd 300 -> 288                  (96%)   <- the edge
--   cmd 320 ->  42                  (13%)   rubber-banded, SLOWER than 220
--   cmd 430 ->   0                          KILLED
--
-- So the old "320 is the measured ceiling" was wrong in both directions: 320 is already
-- over the line, and 220 was leaving 39% on the table. The slider max is now 300 so the
-- rubber-band zone cannot be dialled in by hand.
FM.Speed     = 300        -- ground speed; 288 studs/s actual, 96% of the server's budget
FM.Stick     = 18         -- downward bias, studs/s, to guarantee floor contact
-- Close enough to be standing INSIDE the egg, and that is not a preference - it is what
-- makes the game hand you the right prompt.
--
-- Measured: egg slots sit 6.7 studs apart at the closest, median 7.2, and the prompt sits
-- on the egg (0.97 studs off it, median). Roblox activates the CLOSEST eligible prompt, so
-- at the old 5 stud reach a neighbour could be 6.7 - 5 = 1.7 studs away - nearer than the
-- egg being aimed at - and you would take the wrong one. That is the whole bug.
--
--   stand 5.0 away -> nearest neighbour >= 1.7   (loses, frequently)
--   stand 1.5 away -> nearest neighbour >= 5.2   (wins by 3.5 studs)
FM.Reach     = 1.5        -- start pressing at this range (fixed, no slider)
FM.GrabHold  = 1.3        -- fallback hold when the instant patch did not take
FM.Fallbacks = 0
-- 30, not 60. Braking from 60 studs out spent half the approach crawling; the taper is
-- proportional, so a shorter brake keeps full speed for longer and still arrives stopped.
FM.Brake     = 30         -- start slowing this far out, so arrival is a stop not a skid
-- SNAP THE LAST STRETCH instead of crawling it. The brake exists only because the farm
-- ticks at 33Hz: at 288 studs/s that is 8.7 studs per tick, and you cannot land in a 1.5
-- stud window by taking 8.7 stud steps, so the speed had to taper to 14 studs/s and creep
-- in. That creep cost ~0.40s on EVERY grab.
--
-- A CFrame write lands exactly. Measured, in the area, arriving at full 300:
--
--   at-speed snap  8 studs -> landed 0.00 from target
--   at-speed snap 20 studs -> landed 0.00 from target
--
-- so the taper is replaced by one write once the egg is inside the snap window. 12 studs,
-- because a tick moves at most 8.7 and the window has to be wider than that or a tick can
-- step straight over it.
--
-- This is NOT a way to travel faster - the server budgets ~290 studs/s of net displacement
-- and enforces it against CFrame writes exactly as hard as against velocity:
--
--   single hop  60 studs -> HELD, 0.00 error      (fits inside one window's budget)
--   single hop 150 studs -> REVERTED, gained 0
--   single hop 300 studs -> REVERTED, gained 0
--   single hop 600 studs -> REVERTED, and KILLED
--   chained 40 studs every 0.15s (267/s) -> 257 studs/s, clean
--   chained 40 studs every 0.10s (400/s) -> 159 studs/s, reverted
--   chained 100 studs every 0.10s        ->   0 studs/s, fully blocked
--
-- The header's old claim that 100/200/300 stud hops held was measured at spawn, which is
-- outside the check - the same scoping the airborne note describes. In an egg area they
-- do not hold. So: snap only the final approach, never the journey.
FM.Snap      = 12         -- within this many studs, stop tapering and write the position
FM.SnapOn    = true       -- off falls back to the old taper
FM.Snaps     = 0
-- The free 100 studs at each end of a run. See the hop gate for the measurements; the
-- short version is that standing still banks displacement budget and one hop spends it,
-- so the two moments the farm is already stationary - pressing E, and handing the egg
-- over - each buy a 100 stud jump that costs nothing. 0.35s a leg, two legs an egg.
-- OFF. It works - 0.27s a leg, measured - but it fires at the same instant as the safe
-- zone touch and the touch is the thing that matters. Speed is not worth spending the
-- escape's moment on.
-- Headstart Hop removed entirely - no toggle, no call sites, no flags. hopToward and the
-- movement-budget history it uses are kept: the safe zone touch is built on the same
-- measurements, and _G.SEHUB_HOP stays exposed for testing from the console.
-- Cleared rather than left: _G.FARM survives a reload, so a field from a previous build
-- lingers in it forever and reads like a live setting when someone dumps the table.
FM.Headstart, FM.Headstarts, FM.outboundHop = nil, nil, nil
-- Big eggs. A carried egg is welded into your physics assembly, so a wide one is dragged
-- through the world with you and the server rejects the result; measured 32 studs/s at
-- cmd 300 for a 13.5 wide egg against 220 for a 1.7, ending in a death. Anything over
-- BigWidth is carried at BigCarrySpeed instead of fighting for a speed you cannot have.
-- Drop every competing prompt out of range for the moment of the press, so a big
-- neighbour cannot take the grab. Needs Instant Steal on, since that is what owns the
-- prompt writes. See suppressOthers.
FM.Suppress      = true
FM.BigWidth      = 6      -- hitbox studs above which an egg counts as "big"
-- 280, matching the slider. These two have to agree: this is what the farm starts with,
-- the slider is what the menu hands back, and when they disagreed the declared value won
-- on load and the menu quietly showed a number the farm was not using.
FM.BigCarrySpeed = 280    -- what to ask for while carrying one
FM.RandomPick    = nil    -- removed; the farm no longer reads it
FM.carryW        = 1      -- width of the egg currently in hand
FM.AfterSteal = 0.4       -- settle time after a grab before the run home begins
-- Rally point: where to go first after dying. Respawning puts you at your base, and
-- setting off for an egg from there drags you through whatever geometry is in the way,
-- which is how you end up stuck on a wall. Default is the spot confirmed to have a
-- clean run out; the button re-records it wherever you are standing.
FM.RallyReach = 14
FM.ZoneReach = 12         -- close enough to the delivery point to count as arrived
FM.DepositWait = 20       -- give up on a delivery that never registers, in seconds
FM.BossDist  = -1
FM.BossSpeed = 0
FM.depositAt = nil
-- ONE position for everything, taken from the safe zone. There used to be a second,
-- FM.Home plus a 35 stud offset, invented so the escape would land short and leave a
-- stretch to run in on. It teleported into somewhere lethal, and because the manual key
-- used AB.Zone while the automatic escape used this, the two behaved differently and
-- only the automatic one killed you. Everything now reads the same value the F key
-- reads, so they cannot disagree again.
FM.Home      = nil        -- resolved from AB.Zone at use time; see homePos()
-- Fixed, no sliders. The panel was deliberately cut back to Run Speed, Escape Delay and
-- Fallback Hold; when the boss detection was rebuilt on movement these two came with it
-- and put the clutter straight back. They are tuning constants, not settings.
FM.BossRange   = 140      -- how close a waking guard has to be to count as yours
-- 75, not 45. A jump leaves the ground at roughly 50, so 45 caught every one of them.
FM.FlingLift   = 75       -- upward studs/s that means something threw you
FM.needRally = false
FM.slotCount = nil        -- last seen egg-slot count, for spotting the night reload
FM.Reloads   = 0
FM.Cooldown  = 6          -- seconds before the same prompt is worth trying again
FM.RarePri   = false
-- What happens when Rare Priority is OFF, and it is not optional any more. Nearest-first
-- walks the same short loop round the same few slots; random spreads the run across the
-- map. Sticky per target, area picked first so a dense area does not dominate the draw.
-- A rift-wanted egg still outranks it.
FM.randomKey  = nil
FM.randomArea = nil
-- Friend Drop: let go of the egg near the safe zone instead of banking it, so somebody
-- else can take it. DropRange is how short of the delivery point to stop.
FM.FriendDrop = false
FM.DropRange  = 55
FM.Drops      = 0
-- How many studs of travel one tier of rarity is worth.
--
-- This was 20000, which is wider than the map (about 4600 studs corner to corner), so
-- rarity was absolute: a single Secret anywhere outranked every other egg in the world, the
-- farm crossed the map for it, took the 6 second cooldown, and crossed back. With Prehistoric
-- running Rare through Eternal while Forest runs Common through Legendary, that meant the
-- farm effectively lived in one area - which is exactly what you saw.
--
-- At 400 a tier is worth 400 studs of walking, so rarity still decides between eggs in the
-- same area and distance still decides whether a distant one is worth the trip. Push the
-- slider to the top for the old absolute behaviour.
-- 1500, not 400. At 400 a Common ten studs away beat a Secret three thousand away
-- (-390 against -200), which is the farm ignoring Rare Priority as far as anyone watching
-- is concerned. The map is about 4600 studs corner to corner, so 1500 means rarity always
-- decides between eggs in the same area - where the gaps are tens of studs - while crossing
-- the whole map still needs a gap of three tiers or more to be worth it.
-- 10000, and that is a DELIBERATE return to strict.
--
-- At 1500 one tier was worth 1500 studs, so distance could outrank rarity across tiers
-- and the farm did this, logged live:
--
--   Sand Spider           rank 6    392 studs   <- taken first
--   DeathstalkerScorpion  rank 6    409 studs   <- taken second
--   Bronto                rank 7   2255 studs   <- taken third
--
-- Two Mythics before a Cosmic, because the Cosmic was far. That is "rarest first" only if
-- you squint, and it is not what the toggle says it does - the tooltip has always promised
-- distance breaks ties INSIDE a tier, not across them.
--
-- The map is about 4600 studs corner to corner, so any weight above that makes the ladder
-- absolute: rank decides, distance only ever separates eggs of the SAME rank. 10000 has
-- margin and is not so large that the numbers stop being readable in the diagnostics.
--
-- This was 20000 once and got dialled down because it produced "tunnel vision" - always
-- the same area. That is not a bug in the weight, it is what rarest-first means when one
-- area holds the rarest eggs. It is now a slider rather than a constant, so it can be
-- softened without editing the file.
FM.RareWeight = 10000
-- SIZE PRIORITY. The other way to order a run: biggest egg first instead of rarest.
